<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1527">1527</a>–<a href="/years/1603">1603</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">House of Mecklenburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg, was administrator of the Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin from 1550 and ruled Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> from 1555 until his death in 1603. A Lutheran prince, he introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the bishopric, which in practice became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> of the Mecklenburg house. He supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock">University of Rostock</a> and rebuilt Güstrow Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> style. His daughter Sophia became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-mecklenburg.jpg" alt="Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Theodor Fischer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ulrich3mecklenburg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ulrich was the third son of Duke Albrecht VII of Mecklenburg and Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. He was educated at the Bavarian court and studied theology and law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>. After his father's death he lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/butzow">Bützow</a> and in 1550 followed his cousin Duke Magnus III of Mecklenburg-Schwerin as administrator of the bishopric. He married Magnus's widow Elizabeth, a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-denmark">Frederick I of Denmark</a>, and after her death Anna, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-i-duke-of-pomerania">Philip I, Duke of Pomerania</a>. After the death of his uncle Henry V he took part in governing Mecklenburg, including during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>On 17 February 1555 Ulrich succeeded his brother John Albert I. In 1556 the two divided the country while keeping a common government: Ulrich took the eastern part with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, John Albert the western part with Schwerin. After his brother's death in 1576 Ulrich became guardian of his children, among them John Albert II, who later succeeded him in Güstrow. Ulrich made the castle at Güstrow his main residence.</p><p>A devout Lutheran and an educated prince, he took part in the scientific exchange of his time with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tycho-brahe">Tycho Brahe</a> and David Chytraeus and corresponded with humanists such as Heinrich Rantzau. In 1594, as head of the Lower Saxon Circle, he organised military and financial help against the Turkish threat, and he mediated between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-of-denmark">Frederick II of Denmark</a> and Duke John Adolf of Holstein-Gottorp. He left a fortune of about 200,000 guilders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His granddaughter Anne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI of Scotland</a>. In 1598, when James sought support for his claim to the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Ulrich answered on 20 August with cautious backing, advising that Elizabeth should first name him her successor and making no firm pledge of arms. Ulrich died on 14 March 1603. Through his daughter he was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-iv-of-denmark">Christian IV of Denmark</a> and the great-grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I of England</a>; his grandson Prince Ulrich of Denmark followed him as administrator of Schwerin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 16th century the see was held by princes of the Mecklenburg house, such as <b>Ulrich of Mecklenburg</b>, and became Lutheran, so the bishopric functioned in practice as a Mecklenburg appanage.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-schwerin">Prince-Bishopric of Schwerin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Schwerin, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.63,11.42&amp;year=1576" aria-label="Schwerin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ulrich, Duke of Mecklenburg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5575 5494h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5575 5494h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.63,11.42&amp;year=1576">What was here in 1576? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
